## Finding code
- Already know the file? Open it directly.
- Unfamiliar area? Read `CODEMAP.md` instead of listing or opening many files.
- Don't check or rebuild the map during a task. Run `/map` at the end only if you
  added, moved or deleted files.

## Who does what
- Main session: plan, decide, check the result. Do simple, known things yourself.
- `explorer` (Haiku): reading that needs judgement across 3+ files, long logs or large
  outputs. A search one grep pattern can answer stays in the main session. Check the lines
  `explorer` cites before answering; split a very broad search across 2-3 calls in one message.
- `builder` (Sonnet): small edits of 1-2 files.
- `debugger` (Sonnet): root cause when a failure isn't obvious.
- `researcher` (Sonnet): web and docs lookups.
- `reviewer` (Haiku): review changes over ~30 lines or touching security, data or money.
- `big-decision` (Fable, rare): costly-to-reverse choices, or after two failed attempts.

## Every change
- Do only what was asked, in the fewest lines that fully solve it.
- Before adding a file, function or dependency, search for one that already exists.
- After a change, run the narrowest test that proves it. Two failed fixes → `debugger`.
- Replies: the answer, then at most one short follow-up line.
- Same correction twice, or "always/never": add a one-line rule to `.claude/LEARNED.md`.
- Never read or print secrets. Never commit `.env`, keys or tokens.

Skills with more detail: `lean-code`, `reuse-first`, `self-check`, `compact`, `learn-corrections`.

